# j(qᵈ) as a ratio of weight-12 forms on Γ₀(N)

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.exists_modularForm_mul_qExpansion_eq_coeffEmb_qExpand_jq`
(`a5f4f1c9-dd49-5e6b-a2ab-eaf8e3b2d38c`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `14ce9729-f5e0-5fc0-a7d5-d1f8075e28fb`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ and $d$ be nonzero natural numbers with $d \mid N$. Write $\mathrm{jq}$ for the Laurent
series over $\mathbb Q$ given by $q^{-1}$ times the power series $\mathrm{jNumQ}$ obtained from the
integral series $\mathrm{jNum}$ by base change along $\mathbb Z \to \mathbb Q$, let
$\mathrm{qExpand}\ \mathbb Q\ d$ be the ring endomorphism of $\mathbb Q((q))$ that reindexes a
series along multiplication by $d$ on the exponent group $\mathbb Z$ (the substitution $q \mapsto
q^d$), and let $\mathrm{coeffEmb}\ \mathbb C : \mathbb Q((q)) \to \mathbb C((q))$ be the
coefficientwise ring homomorphism induced by $\mathbb Q \to \mathbb C$. The assertion is that there
exist a weight $k \in \mathbb Z$ and modular forms $g, h$ of weight $k$ for $\Gamma_0(N)$, with $h
\neq 0$, such that in $\mathbb C((q))$ the product of $\mathrm{coeffEmb}\ \mathbb
C\,(\mathrm{qExpand}\ \mathbb Q\ d\ \mathrm{jq})$ with the $q$-expansion of $h$ at $i\infty$ of
width $1$ (Mathlib's `qExpansion 1`, a power series viewed inside $\mathbb C((q))$) equals the
$q$-expansion of $g$ of width $1$. The conclusion is stated in cleared-denominator form, as an
equality of products rather than as a quotient, and the weight is only asserted to exist (the proof
produces $k = 12$).

This records that $j(q^d)$ for $d \mid N$ lies in the field generated by $q$-expansions of modular
forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ of a common weight, classically through $j(d\tau) =
E_4(d\tau)^3/\Delta(d\tau)$. It feeds the study of the Laurent-series realisation of functions on
$X_0(N)$, being used in the identification of the base-changed Laurent field, in the behaviour of
orders of $q$-expansions under slash operators, and in a limit/realisation statement for elements
all of whose orders vanish.

